================================================================================
THE GRID, AND WHAT IT SETTLES
================================================================================

  cell   codec              transport      what it is
  A      Google.Protobuf    grpc-dotnet    R14's baseline, what ArmoniK runs
  B      Google.Protobuf    core (tonic)   README section 13's outcome 2
  C      core-ffi           core (tonic)   both halves of the proposal
  D      core-ffi           grpc-dotnet    the codec alone

All four in ONE process against ONE server in ONE sitting, because B-A and C-B
are subtractions and a subtraction across sittings is worth nothing here.

The cells MIRROR each other, which is what makes the subtraction legal. A parses
straight out of gRPC's `ReadOnlySequence` with no flatten, because that is what
`Grpc.Tools` emits; B parses straight out of the core's buffer with no copy. D
flattens into a reused array because the facade's `Dec` is over `byte[]`; C
copies out of the core's buffer into the same reused array for the same reason.


1. **THE TRANSPORT IS WHERE THE WIN IS, AND IT IS NOT CLOSE.**

B/A, the same codec over each transport, CPU per call, three runs (two forward,
one reversed):

    in flight        1              8             16
    B/A          0.760          0.879          0.583
                 0.804          0.710          0.573
                 0.841          0.800          0.561

**The core's transport costs 16 to 24 percent less CPU per call at 1 in flight,
12 to 29 percent at 8, and 42 to 44 percent at 16** -- and the 16-in-flight
column is the tightest of the three, agreeing to a hundredth across two orders
and three runs. The advantage GROWS with concurrency, which is the opposite
shape from a fixed per-call saving.

C/A, both halves together, is 0.53 to 0.79 at 16 in flight.


2. **THE CODEC HALF IS AT THIS HARNESS'S NOISE FLOOR IN A REAL RPC, AND THE
   QUESTION THE GRID WAS BUILT TO ANSWER CANNOT BE ANSWERED AT THIS PRECISION.**

D/A, the codec alone over grpc-dotnet, is 0.82 to 1.04 -- it straddles 1.0, which
is stage 15's finding (about 10 percent of CPU per call) re-measured.

The subtraction, CPU us per call:

    in flight   B-A (transport)   C-B (codec, core)   D-A (codec, grpc)
    forward  1         -1166.7              -300.0              -466.7
    forward  8          -574.3              +337.8              +168.9
    forward 16         -3159.7              +312.5              -694.4
    reversed 1          -900.0              -200.0              -433.3
    reversed 8         -1587.8               -33.8             -1013.5
    reversed 16        -3263.9              -243.1              -902.8

**C-B and D-A change SIGN with the arm order.** Both are inside the
round-to-round spreads (4 to 27 percent). So whether they agree -- whether the
two halves of the proposal are additive -- is not answerable here, and saying
they agree or disagree from these numbers would be reading noise.

**What IS answerable, and it is the report-level point.** |B-A| is 574 to 3,264
us a call. |C-B| never exceeds 338 and |D-A| never exceeds 1,014. **The transport
half is three to ten times the codec half, and the codec half is at the noise
floor.** A report that presents the proposal as two comparable halves overstates
the codec on .NET. Cell B alone -- README section 13's outcome 2, keeping
`Google.Protobuf` and taking only the core's transport -- gets 12 to 44 percent
of the CPU, which is most of what cell C gets.


3. **THE THREE DELIVERIES ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE IN CPU, AND THE CHECK-IN ASKED
   FOR THAT TO BE SAID PLAINLY IF IT CAME OUT, SO: IT CAME OUT.**

Cell C, CPU us per call, forward then reversed:

    in flight     callback     blocking        queue     spreads
    1               3400.0       3533.3       3600.0     +9/+16/+8%
    8               4527.0       4425.7       3918.9     +4/+8/+10%
    16              4722.2       4062.5       4097.2     +4/+4/+9%
    1 (rev)         3500.0       3433.3       3433.3     +5/+12/+14%
    8 (rev)         3851.4       3614.9       3918.9     +11/+28/+21%
    16 (rev)        4131.9       3923.6       4097.2     +12/+4/+26%

Every difference is inside the spreads. **The callback's one reverse crossing is
free on this host, exactly as the 7.5-to-12 ns calibration predicts, and so is
the CLR attaching a tokio worker thread to run it.** But the queue is equally
free, so **.NET is indifferent between the two non-blocking deliveries** -- it
does not specifically need the callback. That is a cheaper conclusion for the ABI
than the one the check-in expected: if the JVM prefers the queue and .NET is
indifferent, one non-blocking mode would serve both.

Caveat on the queue: this arm runs ONE drainer, as section 9 specifies. A single
drainer at a higher call rate is a bottleneck this arm does not reach.


4. **AND THEN THE CPU COLUMN IS HIDING SOMETHING, WHICH IS WHY THE BLOCKING
   DELIVERY IS NOT MERELY INELEGANT ON .NET.**

The blocking row's CPU is normal. Its WALL CLOCK stalled twice during the grid
runs -- 208 ms and 24 ms a call -- and never in the callback or queue rows. Two
observations is an anecdote, so `--park` is the measurement: one batch of 16
concurrent calls from a COLD thread pool, six runs.

    batch                                        wall ms for 16 calls
    blocking, COLD pool                 8346  2835  2535  3738  4591  3652
    callback, pool already grown          13    14    12    15    13    11
    blocking, pool already grown           8  2987  1990  2985   990  2988
    blocking, SetMinThreads(24)            8    11     8    12     9     8

**Sixteen concurrent blocking calls take 2.5 to 8.3 SECONDS for work the callback
delivery does in 11 to 15 milliseconds: 190 to 630 times.** A host thread parked
in `ak_call_unary` is a thread the pool cannot reuse, and .NET grows its pool past
its minimum at about one or two threads a second. `SetMinThreads` removes it
entirely and nothing else does.

**It is NOT a one-off a long-lived process pays once.** That is what this harness
asserted in its own output before the measurement, and the third row refutes it:
the same batch again, with the pool already grown, is slow in five of six runs,
because the pool retires idle threads between batches. A bursty caller pays it
again and again.

So the argument for section 9's non-blocking delivery on .NET is not the JVM's
argument re-stated. There is no carrier to pin. The cost is the thread pool, it
is 190 to 630x in latency while the CPU column shows nothing, and the only
alternative fix -- `ThreadPool.SetMinThreads` -- is a global setting a library has
no business changing on its host's behalf.


5. **A HAZARD THIS SLICE PREDICTED THAT DOES NOT EXIST HERE, AND WHY.**

The check-in warned about the delegate-rooting hazard this slice found in its own
floor analysis: a callback the collector reclaims while the core still holds it.
**It does not apply to the target binding.** `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]` does not
create a thunk -- the method is compiled to a native entry point at build time
and `&OnDone` is its address, so there is nothing to collect. The hazard is
`Marshal.GetFunctionPointerForDelegate`, whose thunk lives exactly as long as the
delegate object, and that is what a net48 floor binding would have to use. So the
rooting rule is a FLOOR rule, not a .NET rule, and it belongs beside the other
floor findings rather than in the ABI's contract.

The one real trap is the other direction: the completion fires on a tokio worker,
so a continuation scheduled inline would decode 540 KB on a thread the core needs
for its own I/O. The `TaskCompletionSource` is created with
`RunContinuationsAsynchronously` for that reason and the callback copies three
words and returns.


6. **CORRECTNESS, BEFORE ANY OF THE ABOVE.**

Eight cells (four of the grid, plus blocking and queue for B and C), one call
each, the decoded value re-encoded and compared with the exact wire the server
holds -- the facade cells by the managed codec, the incumbent cells by
`ToByteArray`. 8 of 8 byte-identical, first attempt, including the core's dial of
the Unix socket.


================================================================================
WHAT IS NOT MEASURED
================================================================================

  * Whether C-B and D-A agree, per finding 2. More rounds do not fix a
    difference that changes sign with arm order.
  * The upstream direction. Every cell decodes; the request is empty.
  * More than one queue drainer, and the rate at which one becomes a bottleneck.
  * Cancellation, deadlines, metadata, status codes, TLS, streaming over the
    core's transport.
  * `ak_call_unary`'s missing handle: section 9 gives the blocking form one so it
    can be cancelled, and the core's own comment records that stage 4 did not
    build it.
  * A floor binding of the transport. net48 has neither `LibraryImport` nor
    `UnmanagedCallersOnly`, so finding 5's rooting rule would bind there and this
    arm does not build it.
  * Absolutes. R13: this container's numbers are instrumentation.


